#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129184 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc25edf-9d32-5b47-ae4f-e370d2d0d024" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points at Which Tangent Planes Have Particular Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4831fde7-328b-5f95-8b4f-bd555404f79f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263630} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9d3b86-7aec-5970-92c9-47d3d544786f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263795} {:answer/id #uuid "94d3b3cb-73a3-5f94-abc9-28adc6612ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264280} {:answer/id #uuid "9d20a40f-b237-5738-bfa6-76f889ea8789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{3},-1,\\frac{7}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264351} {:answer/id #uuid "a54e3749-cf7d-5ffc-ac93-9256ee36c08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{3},0,\\frac{1}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d20a40f-b237-5738-bfa6-76f889ea8789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{3},-1,\\frac{7}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264351} :answer-field/id #uuid "38b8a09a-7de4-5c87-b2a5-460a354244c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "12830aeb-cb43-530e-902c-e52ba9d7524f" :question/math-academy-id "q-129557" :question/problem "At which point on the surface $z=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v=⟨4,-2,-1⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=6x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨6x,\\,2y,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨4,-2,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨6x,\\,2y,\\,-1⟩=⟨4,-2,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6cx=4 \\\\ 2cy=-2 \\\\ -c=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n6(1)x=4\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n2(1)y=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}+(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(\\frac{2}{3},-1,\\frac{7}{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129184 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc25edf-9d32-5b47-ae4f-e370d2d0d024" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points at Which Tangent Planes Have Particular Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "464dff10-0c59-5dad-80a1-2ee16d60dbfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\pi ,-\\pi ,2\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321709} {:answer/id #uuid "48223b68-6d40-5d37-8bb5-b27364a9cf80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\pi ,\\pi ,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321721} {:answer/id #uuid "a226c677-0c51-577f-b0f5-1b3064cc6815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pi ,2,-2\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322433} {:answer/id #uuid "adf98dbd-c12a-5550-9749-ddcc015f00fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\pi ,\\pi ,12\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322518} {:answer/id #uuid "d80a3e3a-b37d-53fa-b4ba-7e5d51839fdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,6,8\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a226c677-0c51-577f-b0f5-1b3064cc6815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pi ,2,-2\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322433} :answer-field/id #uuid "5baf0726-2cff-5a6b-904f-2992e085ab37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c9333a9-b5d6-5ec8-b1f3-0c62768fb8b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-37986" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v\\,=\\,⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy) \\\\  & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y,\\,-x,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y,\\,-x,\\,-1⟩=⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2c\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-cy=8 \\\\ -xc=2\\pi  \\\\ -c=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-x(-2)=2\\pi \\,⟹\\,x=\\pi .\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-2$ and $x=\\pi $ into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n2(-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -(-2)y=8\\,⟹\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\pi $ and $y=2$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -(\\pi )(2) \\\\  & =-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector  $v$ at $(\\pi ,2,-2\\pi ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129186 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc56f7a5-65a7-53a9-9eb6-ae7b91a1bdd0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Hyperplane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cf0dc93-d9d4-5f21-8930-dd5004fc42a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-2y-2z-w-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407539} {:answer/id #uuid "5110fa49-48ce-56a6-b93d-b9d08b07b745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+2y+2z-w-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407540} {:answer/id #uuid "728a521d-8a62-5203-8384-b89408abe64b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-2y-2z+w-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407541} {:answer/id #uuid "72b11d02-8ab8-5c20-a244-f44e993b6792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+2y-2z-w+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407542} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e8ca25-16b2-54a8-b96c-ad5665775b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-2y+2z+w-8=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cf0dc93-d9d4-5f21-8930-dd5004fc42a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-2y-2z-w-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407539} :answer-field/id #uuid "75cea473-3250-5a97-b3ad-a51baa102dcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ca7d6fc-55d0-503c-8138-64f03d668ed2" :question/math-academy-id "q-73850" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})$ at the point $(1,-1,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}))=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}))=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}))=-\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1,1) & =\\frac{2(1)}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1,1) & =\\frac{2(-1)}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,-1,1) & =-\\frac{2(1)}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-0 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1,1)+(y+1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,-1,1) \\\\ w & =2(x-1)-2(y+1)-2(z-1) \\\\ w & =2x-2-2y-2-2z+2 \\\\ -2x+2y+2z+w+2 & =0 \\\\ 2x-2y-2z-w-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129186 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc56f7a5-65a7-53a9-9eb6-ae7b91a1bdd0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Hyperplane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62fcc3f3-ddff-52b4-b6ee-b406a0ffec98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi x-\\pi y-z+w+\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340014} {:answer/id #uuid "93a5ca28-3798-5c54-9d92-aa2d41eb6d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi x-\\pi y+z+w-\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340459} {:answer/id #uuid "9f8794dd-7491-5377-b367-530775903444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi x+\\pi y+z-w+3\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340576} {:answer/id #uuid "f6251148-2e5e-526a-995c-c61130894b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi x+\\pi y-z+w-\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341308} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7d3452-6743-53e2-bd9c-8cfa857fa7bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi x+\\pi y+z+w-3\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa7d3452-6743-53e2-bd9c-8cfa857fa7bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi x+\\pi y+z+w-3\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341349} :answer-field/id #uuid "a31f7eac-9e1f-5aaa-97e4-e132312fc936" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de6bd964-56b9-511c-9d67-ed8d06732a08" :question/math-academy-id "q-73851" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)$ at the point $(1,1,\\pi ,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))=yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))=xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,1,\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,\\pi ) & =(1)(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi )=-\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,\\pi ) & =(1)(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi )=-\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,\\pi ) & =(1)(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi )=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-0 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,\\pi )+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,\\pi )+(z-\\pi )\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,\\pi ) \\\\ w & =-\\pi (x-1)-\\pi (y-1)-(z-\\pi ) \\\\ w & =-\\pi x+\\pi -\\pi y+\\pi -z+\\pi  \\\\ \\pi x+\\pi y+z+w-3\\pi  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129184 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc25edf-9d32-5b47-ae4f-e370d2d0d024" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points at Which Tangent Planes Have Particular Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52002c6b-9859-5b7b-a1de-586418b14736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,3,28)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339887} {:answer/id #uuid "551d2228-56ee-5488-91bb-0169794e5cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-3,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339916} {:answer/id #uuid "796a4945-abaa-5bd2-997d-087f2262b1b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,3,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340216} {:answer/id #uuid "8a645bd4-5f4d-5164-aa70-44b211e15ecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-3,0)$ and $(-1,3,28)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340370} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b33a5b-7afb-5095-9268-a98f89267aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-3,0)$ and $(3,1,20)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "551d2228-56ee-5488-91bb-0169794e5cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-3,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339916} :answer-field/id #uuid "e58edcff-ee66-5f0c-86cd-1ec279fff31c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c589653c-432a-5bb8-acb2-94698429fbb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-73857" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z={x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}+6y+10$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}+6y+10) \\\\  & =2x-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}+6y+10) \\\\  & =2y+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2x-2,\\,2y+6,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2x-2=0 \\\\ 2y+6=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=1$ while the second equation gives $y=-3.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=1$ and $y=-3$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz={1}^{2}-2(1)+(-3)^{2}+6(-3)+10=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(1,-3,0).$"}]}
